<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    declare Apple Events
    usage so macOS TCC can present the Automation consent prompt instead of
    silently rejecting the automation. Every approved computer-use input action
    activates its target window via an `osascript` call to System Events (see
    `computer::os::activate_window_impl`); on a packaged, signed build that
    Apple Events send requires this usage-description key in the bundle's
    Info.plist, or the send fails with errAEEventNotPermitted BEFORE the
    approved click/type ever reaches enigo, even when Accessibility permission
    was already granted. tauri-bundler MERGES this file into the generated
    macOS Info.plist (our keys win; it still fills CFBundleIdentifier et al.),
    so this adds only the automation string without disturbing the rest.

    Real-machine verification of the resulting Automation prompt is tracked in
    the real-machine checklist — it cannot run in CI, which
    builds and tests but never packages a signed macOS bundle.

    this copy is LOCALIZED, and
    the i18n catalogs stay authoritative (CLAUDE.md: user-facing strings only
    through src/i18n/{en,zh}.ts). macOS renders the consent prompt itself —
    never the webview — so the catalogs' `settings.
    computerUseMacosAutomationPrompt` is mirrored into per-language
    `InfoPlist.strings` resources (src-tauri/locales/{en,zh-Hans}.lproj,
    generated by scripts/gen-infoplist-strings.mjs, bundled via
    tauri.macos.conf.json's resources map so they land in Contents/Resources
    where the OS looks them up). The string below is the development-region
    FALLBACK for any other language and must stay byte-equal to the English
    catalog copy — tests/frontend/infoPlistStrings.test.ts fails the build on
    any drift, in either file.
  -->
  <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Weft brings the window an approved computer-use action targets to the front (via System Events) before it clicks or types. This runs only for actions you approve while Computer Use is enabled in Settings.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
